theory.intro:
The **midpoint** of an interval is the point exactly halfway between its two endpoints. Its coordinates are the **averages** (means) of the endpoints' coordinates: average the two $x$-values, then average the two $y$-values.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.2]
\usetikzlibrary{decorations.markings}
\tikzset{tickmark/.style={postaction={decorate, decoration={markings,
  mark=at position 0.5 with {\draw[black] (0pt,-4pt) -- (0pt,4pt);}}}}}
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (4,2.6);
\coordinate (M) at (2,1.3);
\draw[thick,blue,tickmark] (A) -- (M);
\draw[thick,blue,tickmark] (M) -- (B);
\fill (A) circle (2.5pt);
\node[below right] at (A) {$A$};
\fill (B) circle (2.5pt);
\node[below right] at (B) {$B$};
\fill[red] (M) circle (2.5pt);
\node[above left] at (M) {$M(\overline{x},\ \overline{y})$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

